\title{Cryptographic Hardness}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{We return to questions of computational complexity. In particular, can we cook up examples #{\mathcal{C}} for which #{\mathcal{C}} is not efficiently PAC regardless of the chosen hypothesis class?}

\transclude{CLT1P}

\p{\strong{Claim.} #{f_N(x) = x^3 \bmod N} is a bijection from #{\mathbb{Z}_N^* = \lbrace i : \gcd(i, N) = 1\rbrace } to itself, in fact a group automorphism under multiplication.}

\p{#{f_N^{-1}} we call a \em{discrete cube root}.}

\p{\em{Proof of claim.} #{|\mathbb{Z}_N^*| = (p-1)(q-1)}. By assumption #{3 \nmid (p-1)(q-1)}, so there exist #{d, k} such that #{3d + k(p-1)(q-1) = 1}. Define #{f_N^{-1}(x) = x^d \bmod N}. Then}

##{\begin{aligned}
f_N^{-1}(x)^3 &\equiv x^{3d} \bmod N \\
&\equiv x^{1 - k(p-1)(q-1)} \bmod N \\
&\equiv x \cdot x^{-k(p-1)(q-1)} \bmod N \\
&\equiv x \cdot x^{|\mathbb{Z}_N^*|(-k)} \bmod N \\
&\equiv x
\end{aligned}}

\p{since #{\mathbb{Z}_N^*} is a group. #{\square}}

\p{So given #{p, q}, solving the discrete cube root problem is easy. However, if you are only given #{N} and #{f_N}, computing the discrete cube root is likely \em{very hard}.}

\transclude{CLT1Q}

\p{\em{Idea.} Can we use DCRA to build PAC problems that are intrinsically hard?}

\p{For each #{n \geq 1}, let #{\mathcal{F}_n} be the set of all discrete cube roots for integers #{N = p \cdot q} such that #{n = \log N = } number of bits of #{N}.}

\transclude{CLT1R}

\p{Some thoughts:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{1.} If there were a way to encode DCRA into a learning framework, one would expect our samples to look something like #{(x, f_N^{-1}(x))}. Is this a problem? No! The pair #{(x, f_N^{-1}(x))} can be thought of as #{(y^3, y)}.}
\li{\strong{2.} As stated, computing discrete cube roots is a matter of inputting #{2} integers and outputting an integer. To this point PAC is about inputting labelled samples and outputting yes/no.}
}

\p{All this being said, we still haven’t encoded the DCRA into a PAC problem.}

\transclude{CLT1S}

\transclude{CLT1T}

\transclude{CLT1U}

\transclude{CLT1V}

\p{Given any Boolean circuit #{\mathcal{B}}, there is an associated Boolean function}

##{\Phi_{\mathcal{B}} \colon \lbrace 0, 1\rbrace ^n \to \lbrace 0, 1\rbrace}

\p{that plugs in #{0}’s and/or #{1}’s based on the input to the various indegree #{0} vertices, and propagates to the sink vertex using the labels.}

\p{For a given polynomial #{P}, we write}

##{\mathcal{C}_n^P = \lbrace \text{Boolean circuits with at most } P(n) \text{ many vertices}\rbrace .}

\p{Usually this polynomial is unimportant, and so we say #{\mathcal{C} = \bigcup_n \mathcal{C}_n^P} is a class of \em{polynomial sized circuits}.}

\transclude{CLT1W}

\p{I.e. we showed learning discrete cube roots “reduces” to learning polynomial Boolean circuits…}
